.TH MEDIAGREP 1 "October 2026" "mediagrep @PROJECT_VERSION@" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
mediagrep \- search the metadata of media files, like grep
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mediagrep
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.B \-e
.I EXPR
.RB [ \-e
.IR EXPR " ...]"
.IR PATH ...
.br
.B mediagrep
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.I PATTERN
.IR PATH ...
.br
.B mediagrep \-\-fields
.I FILE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mediagrep
reads the metadata of each media file with MediaInfoLib (title,
publisher, duration, resolution, codecs, languages and so on) and
selects the files that match, the way
.BR grep (1)
selects lines.
For each match it prints the path, the field and its value:
.PP
.RS
.nf
talk.mkv: General/PUBLISHER: Interesting Engineering
.fi
.RE
.PP
In the second form,
.I PATTERN
is a regular expression (ECMAScript syntax) searched for in every field
of every track, except the fields that merely repeat the file's path.
In the first form, each
.I EXPR
is an expression as described under
.BR EXPRESSIONS ;
several are ORed together.
.PP
Directories are searched only with
.B \-r
or
.BR \-R ;
otherwise a directory
.I PATH
is reported as
.I "Is a directory"
and skipped.
Files that MediaInfoLib does not recognize as media are skipped
silently.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-e " EXPR"
An expression. Repeatable; the expressions are ORed.
.TP
.B \-i
Ignore case, in regular expressions and in equality alike.
Only ASCII letters are folded.
.TP
.B \-v
Select files that do not match.
.TP
.B \-l
Print only the paths of matching files.
.TP
.B \-L
Print only the paths of media files that do not match.
With
.BR \-v ,
the files that match.
.TP
.B \-c
Print a count of matching files for each
.IR PATH .
.TP
.B \-0
End output records with NUL instead of newline, for
.BR "xargs \-0" .
.TP
.B \-r
Recurse into directories, not following symbolic links.
.TP
.B \-R
Recurse into directories, following symbolic links.
.TP
.BI \-\-include " GLOB"
Search only files whose name matches
.IR GLOB .
Repeatable.
.TP
.BI \-\-exclude " GLOB"
Skip files whose name matches
.IR GLOB .
Repeatable.
.TP
.BI \-j " N"
Read
.I N
files at a time. The default is the smaller of 2 and the number of
CPUs, since disks and networks, not processors, are the limit.
.TP
.BI \-\-parse\-speed " F"
MediaInfoLib's parse speed, from 0 to 1. The default, 0, reads headers
only; raise it for fields that need the whole file.
.TP
.BI \-\-fields " FILE"
List every field of
.I FILE
with its value, and exit. This is how to find what a field is called.
.TP
.B \-q
Print nothing. Exit with status 0 at the first match.
.TP
.B \-s
Do not report unreadable files.
.TP
.B \-\-progress
Show progress on stderr. On a terminal this is a single line redrawn in
place: files done out of files found, then a percentage, bar and time
remaining once every file has been found. This is the default when
stderr is a terminal and
.B \-q
is not given. When stderr is not a terminal, a line is written every
10 seconds and a summary at the end.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-progress
Never show progress.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show a summary of the options.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Show the versions of mediagrep and of the MediaInfoLib it loaded.
.SH EXPRESSIONS
.nf
expr   := or
or     := and ( "or" and )*
and    := not ( "and" not )*
not    := "not" not | atom
atom   := "(" expr ")" | "pattern" | field | field OP value
field  := [ Track [ "[" N "]" ] "/" ] Name
OP     := = | != | ~ | !~ | < | <= | > | >=
.fi
.PP
Tracks are
.BR General ", " Video ", " Audio ", " Text ", " Image ", " Menu
and
.B Other
from MediaInfoLib, plus
.B File
and
.B Path
from mediagrep. A field without a track is looked up in every track;
.B Audio[2]/Language
looks only in the second audio track. Field names are case-insensitive.
.PP
A comparison is true if any track in scope satisfies it, so
.B "not Audio/Language = ja"
means that no audio track is Japanese. A field on its own tests that it
exists.
.PP
Fields that MediaInfoLib reports as numbers compare numerically.
Durations accept
.BR 1h30m ", " 90m ", " 45s " and " 500ms ;
a bare number is seconds. Sizes and bit rates accept
.BR K ", " M ", " G " and " T
(decimal); a bare number is bytes or bits per second.
Dates compare at the granularity of the operand:
.BR "= 2024" ,
.BR ">= 2024-03" .
Other values compare as text:
.B =
and
.B !=
exactly,
.B ~
and
.B !~
as unanchored regular expressions.
Quote values that contain spaces with
.B \(dq
or
.BR \(aq .
.SS "The File and Path tracks"
.B File
holds what
.BR stat (2)
reports:
.BR Name ", " Directory ", " Extension ", " Size ", " Modified ,
.B Created
where the system records it,
.BR Accessed ,
and on POSIX systems
.BR Mode ", " Owner " and " Group .
Times are UTC.
.PP
.B Path
holds what mediagrep recognizes in the file name:
.B When
from a date such as 2024\-03\-01, 20240301, (2019) or a lone year;
.B Season
and
.B Episode
from S01E02 or 1x02, the season also from a "Season 1" directory;
.B Id
from the eleven-character [id] that yt\-dlp appends.
.PP
Bare patterns do not search either track.
.SS Aliases
.TP
.B @when
File/Modified, File/Created, Encoded_Date, Tagged_Date, Recorded_Date,
Released_Date, Original/Released_Date, Mastered_Date, DATE, Path/When.
.TP
.B @who
Performer, Artist, Publisher, Director, Composer, Author, Channel.
.TP
.B @name
File/Name, Title, Movie, Track, Album.
.PP
An alias matches if any of its fields does, and the output names the
field that matched.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 if any file was selected, 1 if none was, 2 if an error occurred.
Failing to write the output, such as to a full disk, is an error and is
reported as "write error".
With
.BR \-q ,
a match gives 0 even if an error also occurred.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
On Unix, file names reach MediaInfoLib through the C library's locale
conversion, so mediagrep selects a UTF-8 locale for
.B LC_CTYPE
itself: the one in the environment, else C.UTF-8, else en_US.UTF-8.
.SH EXAMPLES
Every file in a tree whose metadata mentions a channel:
.PP
.RS
mediagrep \-ril "practical engineering" ~/Videos
.RE
.PP
Long, high-resolution videos:
.PP
.RS
mediagrep \-r \-e \(aqDuration > 1h30m and Video/Height >= 1080\(aq .
.RE
.PP
Move one publisher's videos into its own directory, safely with any
file name:
.PP
.RS
mediagrep \-il0e \(aqartist ~ business.insider\(aq * | xargs \-0 mv \-t "Business Insider/"
.RE
.PP
Anything dated 2024 by any measure:
.PP
.RS
mediagrep \-rl \-e \(aq@when = 2024\(aq .
.RE
.SH NOTES
Regular expressions use the C++ standard library. With GCC's library,
a regular expression is not applied to values longer than 256 KB, and a
warning says so.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR grep (1),
.BR mediainfo (1)
.SH AUTHORS
Tyler MacDonald and contributors.
<https://github.com/postmaxin/mediagrep>
